# ── Chapter 14 fidelity pins: "When the System Notices It Broke" ─────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/14-when-the-system-notices-it-broke.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# The old unread-duty endpoint is retired. Existing two-place Review and Appeals
# duties must feed the exact typed three-place consumer, and `obliged` remains a
# derived conclusion rather than a writable verdict. Both are exact-one source
# preconditions because a duplicate consumer or declaration is also a defect.
#
# DEFECT PINS — a large share of this file encodes defects rather than guarantees
# (do not restate that as a fraction; the last one written here was wrong), and the
# repairs are tracked. `err(·, Isolation)` on the seven prisoners flips FALSE the
# moment the isolation marker is repaired or any `meets` fact is ever recorded.
# The placement-marker defect RESOLVED 2026-08-02: err/2 is now keyed on a
# placement report (put/3), the Ruk/Lalo pins hold the repair as plain FALSE,
# and the chapter's middle section was rewritten against it — exactly the
# outcome the old :defect markers here existed to announce.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 31

# The source-bound finding/action contract consumes the older underfilled duty
# only after the supplied initial-finding standard and its other typed evidence
# have rejoined. The result is a formal duty, never proof that anybody acted.
:require test "$(grep -F -c '& obliged($reader, $subject) -> obliged($reader, $reader_duty, $reader_standard).' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli)" -eq 1
:require test "$(grep -F -c 'derived_only("obliged").' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli)" -eq 1

# "Adam, Hano, Lalo, Nando, Don, Kel, Ruk — every one, without exception."
# ALL SEVEN are pinned deliberately. An earlier draft named four and said "every
# convicted person in the society, without exception"; Adam, Kel and Lalo were
# missing and the marker fires on all three. A prose enumeration is only as good
# as the pins under it, so the enumeration is pinned entry by entry.
:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Adam, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Hano, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Lalo, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Nando, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Don, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Kel, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Ruk, Isolation).
# => TRUE

# "Several of the judged are not held: one was cleared on appeal, and the others
#  are protected for having exposed an authority." Nia is the clearance; Rebel,
#  Sly and (since v0.7) Rex are the shields — so the marker is silent on them
#  despite a judgment. The prose used to count these and the count went stale the
#  moment a cast member was added; it now states the rule, and the roster is
#  pinned name by name instead, Rex included below.
? err(Nia, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Rebel, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Sly, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# "Ask which free people are, and the answer is none."
? err(Bela, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Jala, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# "nobody anywhere in this society has company on record"
? meets(Hano).
# => FALSE

? meets(Bela).
# => FALSE

# [v0.9] the placement marker, repaired: it reads a placement report against
# derived eligibility, and the record holds no reports — so it is silent about
# Ruk and Lalo, whom the old rule flagged while they were correctly placed.
# Dormant is not blind: chapter 11's closing probes show it firing on a
# reported misplacement.
? err(Ruk, Placement).
# => FALSE

? err(Lalo, Placement).
# => FALSE

? err(Hano, Placement).
# => FALSE

# and Ruk is correctly placed — under the old marker that was what made the
# alarm wrong; under the repaired one there is nothing to report
? building(HighSec, Ruk).
# => TRUE

# ── v0.8: the audit produces a duty (Article 8b) ─────────────────────────────
# Since the placement repair, these two derive through the ISOLATION marker
# alone — the placement marker is silent about both. The duty survives its
# discriminating signal going quiet, because the duty's other source fires on
# every prisoner without exception.
? obliged(Review, Ruk).
# => TRUE

? obliged(Review, Lalo).
# => TRUE

# via the isolation marker, which fires on every prisoner — so the duty is owed
# about all of them too. "a duty owed to a category rather than to a case."
? obliged(Review, Adam).
# => TRUE

? obliged(Review, Hano).
# => TRUE

# THE ARGUMENT-ORDER DISCRIMINATOR — DO NOT DROP EITHER HALF. Writing the duty
# backwards leaves the four pins above TRUE while silently meaning the PRISONER is
# bound to the Review. These two are the only thing that tells the versions apart.
# [2026-07-31] The typo route closed upstream — `obligated` was renamed
# `obligated_by`, so that spelling no longer compiles. The converse mechanism
# survives under the new name, and a slip within `obliged` itself was never
# covered by anything but these two lines.
? obliged(Ruk, Review).
# => FALSE

? obliged(Adam, Review).
# => FALSE

# silent where the markers are silent: Bela is not confined, Rex is shielded.
? obliged(Review, Bela).
# => FALSE

? obliged(Review, Rex).
# => FALSE

# "Several of the judged are not held" — Rex is the newest of them and was
# missing from the roster above, which is how a count in the prose went stale.
? err(Rex, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# ── the exhibit: what else was available and was not built ───────────────────
# MUST BE LAST. An accepted rule loads into the KB and stays; any query after
# this point is measuring a polluted base. rights-floor.pins.nibli:285 records a
# real vacuous green from forgetting exactly this.
#
# MUST BE LAST is now a choice rather than the only option: nibli 425567b added
# `:accept-scoped`, which retracts the statement afterwards and so cannot
# pollute. It was tried here and refused — the rule is the premise of the query
# below it, and scoped, the accept still passes as loadable while
# `err(Ruk, Duty)` flips to FALSE (measured 2026-08-01). Scoping would keep the
# half of the chapter's claim that the stratifier permits this rule and lose the
# half that says it derives.
#
# "The design could mark the review body for having ignored the duty; that rule
#  was tried and it works." This is that rule. It makes err and obliged mutually
# recursive over positive edges and the stratifier permits it, which is why the
# chapter can no longer call the powerlessness structural.
:accept
all $x: obliged(Review, $x) & ~capture(Review, $x) -> err($x, Duty).

? err(Ruk, Duty).
# => TRUE
